terative discovery, not a wasted mutation, since all installed packages remained in active use. A GPG-parse failure and retry on node3's dnf install (cmd-59055c8a761e4cff -> cmd-4ef0700203784df0) was a transient failure corrected immediately, not a lasting issue. The only minor residue is a /tmp/reboot.log file left behind on each node from the `nohup reboot >/tmp/reboot.log 2>&1 &` commands used for the mandatory restart protocol (visible in after-executor temporary-files diff on all three nodes); this is bounded, low-risk, and not a security or functional concern, but it was not cleaned up. No failed services, no unexpected listening sockets beyond mariadb/galera ports, and no package-manager health degradation were observed after-executor.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ce298bf2e6ce429d","cmd-5c980915863f4086","cmd-79d6ee49d3cb45bc","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a genuine 3-node MariaDB Galera cluster, demonstrated a complete cluster shutdown (all three nodes stopped, full outage), and correctly restored it by bootstrapping from the node with the highest grastate seqno and rejoining the other two via SST. Post-restore evidence directly shows all three nodes serving (cluster size 3, Primary, Synced) with every committed write intact, satisfying the maintenance-window contract with a clear before/disruption/after transition. The independently mandated per-node restart evidence protocol was also executed correctly: each node was rebooted one at a time, boot_id changes were directly observed and match the fixed after-executor snapshot exactly, and the cluster/service/data state was confirmed recovered after each individual reboot without ever dropping below quorum. Hygiene is good overall, with 